=== Legal ===
The legal requirements for these licence plates are laid down in a federal law titled {{lang|de|Verordnung über die Zulassung von Fahrzeugen zum Straßenverkehr}} (Ordinance on the admission of vehicles for road traffic), or in the shortened version {{lang|de|Fahrzeug-Zulassungs-Verordnung, FZV}}<ref name="FZV">{{Cite web |title=Verordnung über die Zulassung von Fahrzeugen zum Straßenverkehr |url=https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/fzv_2023/index.html#BJNR0C70B0023BJNE008400000 |publisher=Bundesamt für Justiz |date=2023-07-28 |access-date=2024-07-26 |language=de}}</ref> which replaced part of an older law named {{lang|de|Straßenverkehrszulassungsordnung, StVZO}}{{efn|Literally, "Road-Traffic-Admission-Ordinance", cf. [[German compounds]]}} in 2011. The law distinguishes between {{lang|de|Kennzeichen}} meaning a specific combination of letters and digits, and {{lang|de|Kennzeichenschilder}} which are the physical licence plates.<ref name="FZV" />{{rp|§12}} In everyday language, these terms are often replaced indifferently by {{lang|de|Nummernschild}} and rarely is the difference emphasized by restricted use of either {{lang|de|Nummer}} or {{lang|de|Schild}}.

=== Physical characteristics ===
German number plates are rectangular, with standard dimensions {{cvt|520|×|110|mm|frac=8}} for cars, trucks, buses and their trailers. Plates bearing few characters may have reduced length but must retain the size and shape of the characters. Plates in two lines for the said types of vehicles{{efn|As necessary e.g. on certain cars, such as the rear of a [[Volkswagen Beetle]]}} measure {{cvt|340|×|200|mm|frac=8}}. Motorcycles also have plates in two lines but with specific dimensions: Plates for "large motorcycles" (engine displacement over 125&nbsp;cc or power output exceeding {{cvt|11|kW|bhp}}) issued until 2011 combine a size of {{cvt|280|×|200|mm|frac=8}} with characters of the same size as used for car plates, while those issued since 2011 have characters of reduced size and measure either {{cvt|180|x|200|mm|frac=8}} or {{cvt|220|x|200|mm|frac=8}}. Plates for "light motorcycles" (''Leichtkrafträder'' up to 125&nbsp;cc and {{cvt|11|kW|bhp}}) combine a size of {{cvt|255|x|130|mm|frac=8}} with characters of reduced size. This smallest size of plates is also used for agricultural tractors with a maximum speed of {{cvt|40|km/h}} and their trailers and may also be issued as an exception for certain import cars, when a plate of regular size cannot be applied at the available space.

The characters on the licence plate, as well as the narrow rim framing it, are black on a white background.<ref name="FZV" />{{rp|§12(1)}} In standard size they are {{cvt|75|mm|frac=8}} high, and {{cvt|47.5|mm|frac=8}} wide for letters or {{cvt|44.5|mm|frac=8}} wide for digits. The smaller plates bear characters of {{cvt|49|mm|frac=8}} height and {{cvt|31|or|29|mm|frac=8}} width, respectively.<ref name="FZV" />{{rp|Appendix 4}} In the current system, introduced in 1956, they consist of an area code of one, two or three letters, followed by an identifier sequence of one or two letters and one to four digits. The total quantity of characters on the plate must not exceed eight. Identifiers consisting of one letter with one- or two-digit numbers are often reserved for motorcycle use since there is less space for plates on these vehicles, especially before the reduction of plate size to 180&nbsp;×&nbsp;200&nbsp;mm and 220&nbsp;×&nbsp;200&nbsp;mm in 2011.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kennzeichengrößen |trans-title=Sizes of Licence Plates |url=http://www.kennzeichenbox.de/kfz-ratgeber/kennzeichengroessen |website=Kennzeichenbox.de |access-date=2020-11-27 |language=de |date=2020}}</ref>

==== Typeface ====
Modern German plates use a typeface called [[FE-Schrift]] ({{lang|de|fälschungserschwerende Schrift}}, tamper-hindering script). It is designed so that the letter ''P'' cannot be altered to look exactly like an ''R'', and vice versa; nor can the ''F'' or the ''L'' be forged to equal an ''E'', etc. Another feature is the equal width of all characters, different from the old [[DIN 1451]] script which had been in use since the introduction of the current system in 1956. FE-Schrift can be read by [[optical character recognition|OCR]] software for [[automatic number plate recognition]] more easily than DIN 1451.

The present style was introduced in 1994 and became mandatory in 2000, so the number of licence plates issued in the old style has become very rare. As with many plates for countries within the [[European Union]], a blue strip on the left shows a shortened [[list of international vehicle registration codes|country code]] in white text ('''D''' for {{lang|de|Deutschland}} = Germany) and the [[Flag of Europe]] (12 golden stars forming a circle on a blue background).

==== Material ====
Traditionally, German licence plates are made of aluminium sheeting, with embossed characters rising above background level. Over the years, however, various other materials have been used, albeit to a small extent only. Since 2013, a new style of plates made of plastic is allowed.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-11-12 |title=Kfz-Kennzeichen: Neue Nummernschilder aus Plastik |url=https://www.t-online.de/-/66470970 |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=www.t-online.de |language=de}}</ref> They are said to be less sensitive to mechanical damage and to cause less CO<sub>2</sub> emission in their production, but are more expensive.<ref>{{Cite web |title=3D Kennzeichen – Das Original bei Kennzeichenheld.de |url=https://www.kennzeichenheld.de/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=Kennzeichenheld |language=de-DE}}</ref>
