After German reunification in 1990, the DDR plates were soon abolished and the West German system introduced, starting in 1991 and completed in 1993. Even before this transition phase, it could be observed that licence plates in GDR scheme were produced with West German typeface on the respective machinery.

===West Germany===
In July 1956 the current system was introduced in then West Germany, replacing the post-war system.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Auto-Nummern – Damit die Polizei notiert |journal=[[Der Spiegel]] |date=1952-03-19 |issue=12 |pages=10–12 |url=http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/d-21317933.html|access-date=2020-12-18 |trans-title=Car numbers – For the police to write down |language=de}}</ref> The occupation zones were no longer referred to, instead the new system based on the [[districts of Germany]]. Each of these was assigned an alphabetic code which had its origin in the name of the district, i.e. of the city or of the capital of the rural district. Quite often, a "district-free" city was surrounded by, or adjacent to a rural district of the same name. In this case, they would both share the code as well as the name, yet devise a way how to split the possible alphanumerical combinations.

[[File:Numberplate de 1985.jpg|thumb|right|Number plate in the 1956 style, from [[Hannover]]]]
The number of letters in the area code hints at the size of the district. The basic idea was to even out the number of characters on all licence plates, because the most populous districts would have more cars and, consequently, more digits after the prefix. The largest German cities generally only have one-letter codes, such as B=[[Berlin]], M=[[Munich]] ({{lang|de|München}}), K=[[Cologne]] ({{lang|de|Köln}}), F=[[Frankfurt]], S=[[Stuttgart]], H=[[Hannover]]. Therefore, cities or districts with fewer letters are generally assumed to be bigger and more important whereas three-letter codes tend to be regarded as rural and dull. Reflecting that, most districts aimed for a combination with fewer letters for their prefix code.

The most significant exception of the one-letter code is Germany's second largest city [[Hamburg]] which bears HH for {{lang|de|Hansestadt Hamburg}}, because of its historical membership in the [[Hanseatic League]], reflected already in its prefix used between 1906 and 1945. A similar principle applies to [[Bremen]] and [[Bremerhaven]], forming the state [[Bremen (state)|Free Hanseatic City of Bremen]] and sharing the common prefix HB (1906–1947, and again since 1956). Likewise, {{lang|de|Hansestadt}} [[Lübeck]] received its former prefix HL, already used between 1906 and 1937 when its statehood was abolished.

The first drafts, however, had to be altered in a few cases. The district of [[Wittlich]] rejected the code ''WC'', understandably, and received '''WIL''' instead. The code ''KZ'', initially projected for [[Konstanz (district)|Konstanz]], was withdrawn fast, due to recent history, and replaced by '''KN'''. Neither were ''SA'', ''SS'' or ''HJ'' considered to be issued. The code ''SD'' was projected for [[Stade (district)|Stade]] and was finally altered into '''STD''' after protests from that district who did not want to bear the abbreviation of the {{lang|de|[[Sicherheitsdienst]]}}.

When originally planned, the system included [[:de:Ostzonenverzeichnis der deutschen Kfz-Kennzeichen|codes for districts in Eastern Germany]] which were to be reserved until [[German reunification|reunification]]. That included the territory of the [[German Democratic Republic|GDR]] as well as the territories [[Oder-Neisse line|annexed]] to [[Poland]] and the [[Soviet Union]] after [[World War II]], which West Germany's government still claimed in that era until about 1970. When reunification came in 1990, the reserved codes (e.g. ''P'' for [[Potsdam]]) were indeed issued to East German districts in January 1991, often as originally planned and as they existed at that time.

====First changes====
Starting in the early 1970s, West German districts were extensively rearranged.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://amor.cms.hu-berlin.de/~h0598bce/docs/hw2000-local-government-modernisation.pdf |title=Local Government Modernisation in Germany: Stages, discourses, patterns, international perspective. |first=Hellmut |last=Wollmann |page=7 |access-date=2020-11-11}}</ref> In order to reduce their number and so simplify governance, different steps could be taken:
* city districts "swallowed" neighboring municipalities and thus grew.<ref group=example>The city of [[Bielefeld]] incorporated the largest part of the eponymous rural district, almost doubling their population.</ref>
* city districts lost their sovereignty and were integrated into the surrounding or neighbouring rural district.<ref group=example>The district-free towns of [[Marktredwitz]] and [[Selb]] were merged into the district of [[Wunsiedel (district)|Wunsiedel]].</ref>
* rural districts merged with one or several others, or were split up between neighbouring districts.<ref group=example>The district of [[Springe]] split up into the districts of [[Hameln-Pyrmont]], [[Hanover region|Hanover]], [[Hildesheim (district)|Hildesheim]] and [[Schaumburg]].</ref>
* single municipalities were moved between districts, as was deemed appropriate or practical.
In each of these cases, the new districts had to be endowed with an area code. Again, various solutions were possible:
* the largest or most populous district bestowed its name and code upon the newly created unit.<ref group=example>The urban district of [[Schwandorf]] (SAD) merged with the rural districts of Burglengenfeld (BUL), Nabburg (NAB), Neunburg (NEN) and Oberviechtach (OVI) into a new rural [[Schwandorf (district)|district of Schwandorf]] (SAD).</ref>
* one former district gave its name and/or capital while another's area code was used for the new district.<ref group=example>The Rheingaukreis (RÜD for Rüdesheim) and Untertaunuskreis (SWA for Bad Schwalbach) districts merged into [[Rheingau-Taunus-Kreis]], choosing the area code RÜD but the capital Bad Schwalbach.</ref>
* the new unit was given a new name yet continued to use an existing area code.<ref group=example>The districts of Eschwege and Witzenhausen merged into [[Werra-Meißner-Kreis]], using Eschwege's ESW and dropping WIZ.</ref>
* the new unit was given a new name and created a new area code.<ref group=example>The districts of Eiderstedt (TÖN for [[Tönning]]), Husum (HUS) and Südtondern (NIB for [[Niebüll]]) merged into [[Nordfriesland (district)|Nordfriesland]] (NF).</ref>
In any case, the adamant rule was that ''one'' area code per district was valid and would be issued to any vehicle registered henceforth. Existing registrations would remain valid until the vehicle was removed from this district to be either relocated or permanently deregistered. Another rule, however, was abolished. Whereas rural districts had generally been named after their capital town, it was now possible to create new names, applying to geographical<ref group=example>The rural districts of Heidelberg (HD), Mannheim (MA) and part of Sinsheim (SNH) merged into [[Rhein-Neckar-Kreis]] (HD); the districts of Alsfeld (ALS) and Lauterbach (LAT) merged into [[Vogelsbergkreis]] (VB).</ref> or historical features.<ref group=example>Öhringen (ÖHR) and Künzelsau (KÜN) merged into [[Hohenlohe (district)|Hohenlohekreis]] (KÜN).</ref> As well it was possible to combine the names of the districts that had merged, either keeping one of their codes<ref group=example>The rural districts of Straubing and Bogen merged to [[Straubing-Bogen]], keeping SR (and sharing it with [[Straubing]] city district) while dropping Bogen's BOG.</ref> or creating a new one.<ref group=example>The rural districts of Koblenz and Mayen merged to [[Mayen-Koblenz]], newly introducing MYK while dropping MY and leaving KO to the city of [[Koblenz]].</ref>

===Germany reunited===
[[File:Trabant (7909447336).jpg|thumb|right|Trabant registered in Stendal; pre-1994 typeface{{efn|With emission test sticker valid February 2011}}]]
When the GDR ceased to exist and Germany was reunited in its present size on 3 October 1990, new area codes were issued to the East German districts. In many cases they could be taken from the old lists that had been prepared before 1956: '''P''' stood for [[Potsdam]], '''EF''' for [[Erfurt]], '''SON''' for [[Sonneberg]]. Yet, a considerable number of codes was altered, either because a code which had been reserved for a district in today's Poland or Russia had become available,<ref group=example>DZ had once been reserved for [[Gdańsk|Danzig]] but was now issued to [[Delitzsch]].</ref> or because the projected code had meanwhile been issued to a West German district.<ref group=example>[[Gotha]] had been scheduled to receive GT, yet this code went to the newly-created district of [[Gütersloh (district)#History|Gütersloh]] in 1973. [[Gotha (district)|Gotha]] was given GTH instead.</ref>

A prominent example of a reserved code being reused before reunification was one-letter '''L''' which was originally planned for [[Leipzig]], by far the largest German city starting with L. However this code was given to the newly formed [[Hesse|Hessian]] city of [[Lahn, Hesse|Lahn]] and the district [[Lahn-Dill-Kreis]] in 1977, as hopes for reunification faded away. After the rather unexpected reunification (and Lahn city having split up again and thus abolished in 1979), the L was returned to the city of Leipzig and Lahn-Dill-Kreis was issued '''LDK''' instead.

The letter '''G''' was first reserved for the East German city of [[Görlitz]] and later awarded to the city of [[Gera]], although both are smaller than the West German [[Gelsenkirchen]] ('''GE'''). The area code '''ZK''' had been reserved, in the 1950s, for the city of [[Zwickau]] but was rejected as ZK had become the abbreviation of the loathed {{lang|de|[[Socialist Unity Party of Germany#Central Committee|Zentralkomitee]]}} of the former Communist party SED.

In analogy to the three northwestern {{lang|de|Hansestädte}} Hamburg, Bremen and Lübeck, but without historical examples of formerly issued prefixes, four northeastern Hanseatic cities, [[Greifswald]], [[Rostock]], [[Stralsund]] and [[Wismar]], chose the prefixes '''HGW, HRO, HST''' and '''HWI'''. There were no suitable two-letter codes available since '''HG, HR, HS''' and '''HW''' were already taken by West German districts.{{efn|1='''HG''' = [[Hochtaunuskreis]], capital: [[Bad Homburg vor der Höhe]]<br />'''HR''' = [[Schwalm-Eder-Kreis]], capital: [[Homberg (Efze)|Homberg]]<br />'''HS''' = [[Kreis Heinsberg]]<br />'''HW''' = Kreis [[Halle (Westfalen)]].}}

Beginning in the mid-1990s, however, districts in East Germany were rearranged again, similar to the West two decades before. Thus many of these codes issued before were now outdated, but could still be seen alongside the new code.<ref group=example>In [[Torgau-Oschatz]] district, merged in 1994, you might well see the new TO alongside Torgau's TG and Oschatz's OZ, at the same time.</ref> This rearrangement was continued in a second step after 2000, which created large districts with a remarkable variety of possible area codes registered. Still, only one of these was the current one which would be issued to vehicles registered at the moment.

===Liberalized registration rules===
After the reorganization of districts, from the 1970s onward, many area codes expired and new ones were created at that time. However, number plates issued before these rearrangements remain valid, providing the vehicle is still in use and has not been reregistered since. So it was still possible, if rare, to see a [[classic car]] with registration codes of administrative units that have not existed for over 30 years.<ref group=example>EIN = [[Einbeck]], which was merged into [[Northeim (district)|Northeim]] in 1974.</ref> 
[[File:Germany 2020 Ueberlingen license plate.jpg|thumb|right|Überlingen licence plate, reintroduced in [[Bodenseekreis]] in 2020]]
A study conducted in 2010–12 produced the result that 72% of the German population would welcome the possibility to use again these abolished area codes whereas only 13% opposed the idea.<ref name="IKL">{{cite news |last=Bochert |first=Ralf |title=Initiative Kennzeichenliberalisierung |trans-title=Initiative on the Liberalization of Licence Plates |url=https://www.hs-heilbronn.de/kennzeichenliberalisierung-daf96049d3206fa0 |access-date=2020-12-18 |publisher=[[Hochschule Heilbronn]] |date=October 2020 |language=de |archive-date=5 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210505155659/https://www.hs-heilbronn.de/kennzeichenliberalisierung-daf96049d3206fa0 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Kennzeichenliberalisierung |url=http://www.vox.de/cms/kennzeichenliberalisierung-884617.html |access-date=2020-12-27 |work=vox news |date=2011-09-30 |language=de}}</ref> 
It was regarded as remarkable that even young people who had never driven a car with such an "old" prefix favoured the idea of this so-called {{lang|de|Kennzeichenliberalisierung}} (licence plate liberalization).<ref>{{cite news |last=Perske |first=Jörn |title=Nummernschilder-Nostalgie |trans-title=Number plate nostalgia |url=https://www.fr.de/politik/spd-org26325/nummernschilder-nostalgie-11395156.html |access-date=2025-03-18 |work=Frankfurter Rundschau |date=2019-01-21 |language=de}}</ref> For one, their chances at receiving a personalized license plate would improve if the aspired combination was available not once but several times within their district. Yet the main reason mentioned was a pride in their home town and their own roots, in times of globalization.<ref>{{cite news |title="Wir sind keine Separatisten!" – Gute Chancen für Alt-Kennzeichen "HÜN" |trans-title="We're not separatists" – Good chances at old code "HÜN" |url=https://osthessen-news.de/n1205486/h-nfeld--wir-sind-keine-separatisten---gute-chancen-f-r-alt-kennzeichen-h-n-.html |access-date=2025-03-18 |publisher=Osthessen-News |date=2011-11-02 |language=de}}</ref>

The police, however, warned against introducing further codes, as it had turned out that observant citizens would easily notice a car with a number plate from a distant district, thus assisting the police in solving crimes. Plates from the vicinity, on the other hand, would be easier to remember in full, and this would also help to find offenders. More opposition came from local politicians who maintained they had at last succeeded in unifying their merged districts and healed the wounds of those inhabitants who had to give up "their" prefix. If that prefix was available again, they feared, it might lead to old feuds within districts flaming up anew<ref>{{cite news |last=Klein |first=Hendrik |title=50 Jahre Märkischer Kreis: Noch 674 Fahrzeuge mit Altkennzeichen |trans-title=50 years of [[Märkischer Kreis]]: still 674 vehicles with old registration plates |url=https://www.lokaldirekt.de/news/serie-50-jahre-maerkischer-kreis-10-noch-674-fahrzeuge-mit-altkennzeichen |access-date=2025-03-18 |agency=LokalDirekt |date=2025-02-06 |language=de}}</ref> – or, as the district of [[Westerwaldkreis]] put it, "reopen the old trenches between former Oberwesterwaldkreis (WEB) and former Unterwesterwaldkreis (MT)."<ref>{{cite web |title=Kreis lehnt Alt-Kennzeichen MT und WEB ab |trans-title=District rejects old license plates MT and WEB |url=https://www.rhein-zeitung.de/lokales/westerwaelder-zeitung/kreis-lehnt-alt-kennzeichen-mt-und-web-ab_arid-473229.html |website=rhein-zeitung.de |publisher=Westerwälder Zeitung |access-date=2025-04-11 |language=de |date=2012-08-23}}</ref>

Nonetheless, the [[Federal Ministry of Transport and Digital Infrastructure|Federal Ministry of Transport]] complied with the majority of citizens. Beginning in November 2012 in some districts, and meanwhile nationwide, most of these expired prefix codes have been reintroduced, e.g. in the district of [[Wesel (district)|Wesel]], it has again become possible to register vehicles with '''MO''' as used for the former district of [[Moers]] and '''DIN''' as used for the former district of [[Dinslaken]], additionally to the standard '''WES''' which had been the only code issued since 1975. As of September 2024, the liberalization has led to 328 previously abolished codes being reintroduced.<ref name="IKL" />

In 2024, the same college which had initiated this movement suggested a second step. Every town above 20,000 inhabitants should be able to apply for their own code, even if they had never had one and were subordinate municipalities of a district.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bochert |first=Ralf |title=Kennzeichenliberalisierung II. Teil (2024ff) |url=https://cdn.hs-heilbronn.de/f4c979104f86a263/21a48fc0f610/Projektskizze_ProjektteilII |access-date=2024-10-09 |publisher=Hochschule Heilbronn |date=2024-09-26 |language=de}}</ref> The reactions by the political bodies responsible for an implementation of this idea were restrained but varied.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Luca |title=Neue Kennzeichen für rund 320 Städte? |trans-title=New License Plates for about 320 towns? |url=https://www.swr.de/swr-aktuell/baden-wuerttemberg/heilbronn/neue-auto-kennzeichen-fuer-rund-320-staedte-100.html |access-date=2024-10-08 |publisher=[[Südwestrundfunk]] |date=2024-09-24 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Ehm |first=Harald |title=So könnte Zirndorf ein eigenes Autokennzeichen bekommen |trans-title=This is how [[Zirndorf]] could receive their own license plate |url=https://www.nn.de/fuerth/so-koennte-zirndorf-ein-eigenes-autokennzeichen-bekommen-1.14439105?login_success=true |access-date=2024-10-07 |publisher=[[Nürnberger Nachrichten]] |date=2024-10-05 |language=de}}</ref>

===Slackening of rules===
Furthermore, it has become possible to "take one's number along", i.e. to keep a licence plate issued at the previous address after moving away from that district.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Haschek |first1=Brigitte |last2=Cerchez |first2=Stefan |title=Nummernschild zum Mitnehmen |trans-title=Take-away number plate |url=http://www.auto-motor-und-sport.de/reise/pro-und-contra-kennzeichen-liberalisierung-nummernschild-zum-mitnehmen/ |access-date=2020-12-23 |publisher=Auto Motor und Sport |date=2013-07-18 |language=de}}</ref> For that reason, the area code and the respective state seal on a licence plate do not necessarily mean that the vehicle's owner really lives there.

In 2023, the city of [[Munich]] applied for a second code, as the remaining free combinations with '''M''', which the city has to share with the [[Munich (district)|district of Munich]], were becoming scarce. The code '''MUC''', which is also the IATA code for [[Munich Airport]], was granted by the Federal government on 21 September.<ref>{{cite news |author=Bayerische Staatsregierung (Bavarian State Government) |work=Pressemitteilungen |title="MUC"-Kennzeichen kommt! |trans-title="MUC" plate is coming! |url=https://www.bayern.de/muc-kennzeichen-kommt/?seite=2453 |access-date=2023-09-25 |date=2023-09-21 |language=de}}</ref>

==Insurance plates==
[[File:Versicherungskennzeichen.jpg|thumb|Insurance plates; the colour of the letters alternates yearly.]]
[[File:German microcar - right.jpg|thumb|Car with maximum speed reduced to {{cvt|25|km/h|abbr=on}}, hence using an insurance plate{{efn|Additionally, the white sticker reading "25" signals low speed to following drivers.}}]]
[[File:E-Scooter HL Germany.jpg|thumb|E-Scooter]]

'''Light motorised vehicles''' such as mopeds, motorized wheelchairs and other small, low-power vehicles (such as vehicles for the physically handicapped, with a maximum speed of {{cvt|50|km/h|abbr=on}}) are required to have a registration plate of a different kind. This {{lang|de|Versicherungskennzeichen}} (insurance plate) uses a system of three digits on the top and three letters beneath.<ref name=FZV />{{rp|§52}} Both numbers and letters are chosen randomly so personalising the plates is not possible. Plates are much smaller{{efn|Regular size {{cvt|130|×|101|mm|frac=8}}; also available as sticker for [[e-scooters]] in {{cvt|67|×|55|mm|frac=8}}}} than the plates for normal cars and are only valid for one year from 1 March until the end of February the following year.<ref>{{cite news |title=Jetzt Nummernschild wechseln! |trans-title=Change number plate now! |url=http://www.autobild.de/artikel/versicherungskennzeichen-49234.html |access-date=2021-01-02 |work=AutoBild |date=2020-02-26 |language=de}}</ref> Those plates are sold by insurance companies, so the fee includes both the registration and the cost of one year's insurance for the vehicle. There are four colours used: black, blue, green for normal plates, and red for temporary use, such as testing (very rare). The first three colours alternate annually in order to make it easy to see whether the vehicle has the correct plate and insurance.

